#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bd-mutation-test -- would this gate still pass if the feature were DELETED?

WHAT THE OLD ONE DID (<= v3.66.737), and why it was worthless:

    _classify_ok()      <- its OWN reference implementation of the guard
    _invariant_holds()  <- compares each mutant AGAINST THAT SAME REFERENCE

It asked "does a function that differs from my reference differ from my
reference?" -- true by construction, kill-rate 4/4, always. It NEVER OPENED THE
TREE: `--work` was accepted and never used, and it never referenced bd-evidence,
bd-agent-watchdog, bd-gui-surface, bd-netns-proof or bd-tool-lint -- the five
gates it was supposed to audit. DEMONSTRATED: it returns exit 0 and prints
"all mutations caught -- the invariant is discriminating" when pointed at an
EMPTY DIRECTORY, and against a tree with the real SSRF guard gutted.

The tool built to hunt gate-blindness was itself a blind gate. That is the
meta-shape (detector-with-the-bug-it-hunts), and it is why this rewrite exists.

WHAT THIS ONE DOES, for each registry row:

  1. COPY the tree to scratch (a gate must not pollute what it inspects).
  2. BASELINE: run the REAL gate on the pristine copy. It MUST be GREEN, or a
     red afterwards proves nothing -- report BASELINE-RED.
  3. MUTATE the REAL source file: break the actual feature the gate guards.
  4. VERIFY THE MUTATION LANDED (text changed; .py still parses). A mutation
     that never applied and a gate that never noticed are indistinguishable from
     outside, and calling that CAUGHT would be the original sin repeated.
  5. RE-RUN the REAL gate. RED -> CAUGHT. GREEN -> SURVIVED (a finding).
  6. Anything we could not do -> UNKNOWN, and UNKNOWN FAILS.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
